          "inlines": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "text": "If we have two different quadratics, we cannot equate their coefficients since they are not the same quadratic.",
              "marks": []
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "id": "p59-q10-request",
          "type": "paragraph",
          "fontSize": null,
          "align": "left",
          "spaceBefore": 0,
          "spaceAfter": 1,
          "lineHeight": 1.4,
          "indent": 0,
          "inlines": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "text": "However, is there any special relationship between the two sets of coefficients when the two quadratics share the same solutions?",
              "marks": []
            }
          ]
        }
      ]
    },
    "layout": "list",
    "columns": null,
    "questionDiagrams": [],
    "children": [],
    "answer": {
      "short": "For the same root pair, including multiplicities, the coefficients are proportional:\n$$(a_1,b_1,c_1)=\\lambda(a_2,b_2,c_2),\\qquad\\lambda\\ne0.$$\n\nWrite both quadratics using their common root factors. Merely having no real roots in both quadratics is insufficient.",
      "worked": "Let $f(x)=a_1x^2+b_1x+c_1$ and $g(x)=a_2x^2+b_2x+c_2$, with $a_1,a_2\\ne0$. If their common root pair is $r,s$, including $r=s$ for a repeated root,\n$$\\begin{align*}f(x)&=a_1(x-r)(x-s)\\\\g(x)&=a_2(x-r)(x-s).\n\\end{align*}$$\nSet $\\lambda=\\frac{a_1}{a_2}\\ne0$. Then $f(x)=\\lambda g(x)$. Comparing corresponding coefficients gives\n$$\\begin{align*}a_1&=\\lambda a_2\\\\b_1&=\\lambda b_2\\\\c_1&=\\lambda c_2.\n\\end{align*}$$\nThus $(a_1,b_1,c_1)=\\lambda(a_2,b_2,c_2)$. This remains valid when a linear or constant coefficient is zero.\n\nHere, “the same solutions” means the same root pair, including multiplicities. Two quadratics can both have no real solutions without being proportional: $x^2+1$ and $x^2+2$ are an example.",
      "solutionDiagrams": []
    },
    "answerSpaceMm": 28
  },
